We test whether a direction for English human-noun number has corresponding causal effects across twenty pretrained language models. A coordinate estimated from labelled nouns recovers a median 0.85 of the full residual-patch effect on five held-out agreement behaviours. Maps fitted on generic text transport the direction above the specified recovery criterion in 349 of 380 ordered model pairs. Random
directions, shuffled maps and an untrained source provide contrasting controls. Transfer weakens for a different number cue and noun class and, in a one-pair test, for other fitting corpora; ablation decisions depend on the outcome scale. The central result is cross-model causal correspondence on the tested agreement domain. The experiments establish transferable intervention effects and their boundaries;
they do not identify a uniquely shared internal mechanism.
